      "id": "bge18e5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Foundation of the Delhi Sultanate"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Delhi Sultanate was established in 1206 AD by Qutb-ud-din Aibak and lasted until 1526 AD.",
        "Five dynasties ruled sequentially: Slave (Mamluk), Khilji, Tughlaq, Sayyid, and Lodi.",
        "Iltutmish consolidated the empire, organized the Iqta system, and set up the Turkan-i-Chahalgani.",
        "Alauddin Khilji established market price controls, a standing army, and introduced the Dagh and Chehra systems.",
        "Muhammad bin Tughlaq introduced token currency and briefly transferred the capital to Daulatabad.",
        "The Sultanate came to an end in 1526 when Babur defeated Ibrahim Lodi in the First Battle of Panipat."
      ],
      "id": "wdblib2"
    },
